      "writtenOn": "2022-11-14T22:14:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I feel like the example you showed (second one) can be subtly wrong and I disagree with the approach. Namely, my concern stems from the following: awaiting an async block yields to any other active job in the same pool.\n\nThis means that if you wanted to make decisions based on a shared state, you would have to acquire a lock inside the async block for protection. It would be better to instead use a single sync block for all receive operations so that\u0027s not the case. \n\nOur current dispatcher (in stack/lib.rs) already supports this. You can add a function like \"handle_advmon_callbacks\" to do this. So let me suggest the following refactor:\n\n```\nfn start_scan(...) -\u003e GattStatus {\n  ...\n  \n  let next_id \u003d self.get_next_msft_id();\n  let ret \u003d gatt.lock().msft_adv_monitor_add((\u0026next_id, \u0026filter);\n  if ret !\u003d GattStatus::kSuccess {\n    return ret;\n  }\n  \n  self.add_advmon_timeout_for(\u0026next_id, MsftCallbacks::AdvMonitorAdded);\n}\n\nfn handle_advmon_callback(...) {\n  match callback {\n    MsftCallbacks::AdvMonitorAdded(pseudo_id, monitor_id) \u003d\u003e {\n      self.monitors.insert(monitor_id);\n      self.msft_advmon_enable();\n    },\n    \n    MsftCallbacks::AdvMonitorRemoved(monitor_id) \u003d\u003e {\n      self.monitors.remove(monitor_id);\n      if self.monitors.size() \u003d\u003d 0 {\n        self.msft_advmon_disable();\n      }\n    }\n    \n    MsftCallbacks::OnTimeout(pseudo_id, api_called) \u003d\u003e {\n      // Error recovery for call\n    }\n  }\n}\n```",
